    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Oak forests worldwide play a vital role in maintaining species diversity and critical ecosystem processes; yet over the last 40 years, the coverage of oak forests in California has decreased by over 50% due to a combination of extensive browsing, higher rates of desiccation due to climate change, and the rapid spread of a pathogenic disease known as sudden oak death. As a result, efforts to bolster the decreasing rates of regeneration in oaks have become increasingly important. Several scientists and conservationists have tried to remedy the situation by inhibiting browsing.\r\nThis thesis explores the effectiveness of herbivore exclosures in encouraging oak regeneration and sapling recruitment, and presents the results of a meta-analysis of 32 published studies on the subject. The meta-analysis is accompanied by data that were collected in 2016 from an ongoing exclosure experiment at Stanford’s Jasper Ridge Biological Preserve (JRBP). For three sympatric oak species, 75-pairs of seedlings were identified in 2009 and protected from or exposed to deer, a primary herbivore of oak saplings worldwide. The meta-analysis was conducted to assess the impact of this ecological factor –ungulate herbivory– worldwide, and to assess the consistency of those studies with the JRBP data. My results suggest that ungulate herbivory represents an important population regulation factor in oak populations, and that the increased abundance of these herbivores constitutes an ecological bottleneck for the natural regeneration of the populations of these emblematic trees, locally and globally.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
